WEBVTT

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.000
Do you happen to have a match?

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:36.150
Say, where do you come from?

00:01:36.750 --> 00:01:38.270
This building is closed.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:41.390
Thank you.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.320
We'll have you the correct time.

00:01:46.890 --> 00:01:48.070
It is just nine o'clock.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:49.890
Oh, thanks very much.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.320
Still, I don't understand.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:52.920
I don't understand.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.680
I don't know.

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:58.580
Police!

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:29.220
- Death was caused by strangulation, Inspector. - What's your name?

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:31.480
- Luigi Bacigaloup.

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:33.380
- All right, Louie, what do you know about this?

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:34.760
- Me? I don't know nothing.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.410
I only know what I say over the phone.

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:39.080
- Well, let's have your story.

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.080
- Well, I knew something terrible was going to happen tonight.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:44.900
- So you knew something, huh?

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.740
All right, what's the second?

00:03:46.740 --> 00:03:48.690
- Why, there's been a... - Oh, yeah.

00:03:48.690 --> 00:03:57.990
Let's see. Hello, Casey.

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:00.000
- Come on, Casey, go. I got a date with you.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.960
- Sure you have. Only Riley don't know it. - Well, you go on in and tell him. Then he will know it.

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.840
- Come on, scram. - Now, you know Riley always lets me in on a murder.

00:04:08.840 --> 00:04:15.510
- How'd you know this was a murder? - Easy. I made a quick checkup on the population of the city and found out we were one short.

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:24.340
- Yeah? Well, if you don't get out of here, we're gonna be too short. - Aha, you admit it. I'd be victim number two, so who was number one?

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:33.330
Garfield. Garfield.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.630
Where did I read something about him today?

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:37.570
Now, listen here.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:38.090
I remember.

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:42.260
The Garfield Investment Company was petitioning to bankruptcy this morning.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.440
Another stockbroker gone in the way of all flesh.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:49.100
Another stockbroker?

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:53.730
Say, Casey, run in and tell Raleigh I've got to see him.

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:54.430
I've got a clue.

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:54.990
But, uh...

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.590
Come on, now, Casey. Come on.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.000
I'm sorry, sir, but that Burton guy from the Chronicle is upset.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:01.120
You mean inside.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:02.120
Now listen.

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:03.120
All right, Casey.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:04.120
All right, boy.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.120
What are you doing here, Burton?

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:07.120
Well, you know I always tag along with you on all your unsolved murders.

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:09.120
I just thought I'd come along to see that you didn't break the record.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:10.120
Is that so?

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.200
Well, don't break anything on this story till I tell you to it.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:14.200
There'll be trouble.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:15.340
Ah, you know me.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:22.630
All right, Casey.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:24.510
Strangled, huh?

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:25.510
Just like the other cases.

00:05:25.510 --> 00:05:26.510
Yeah.

00:05:26.510 --> 00:05:27.520
That's not the only similarity either.

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.000
When the murderer was leaving the building, he asked the janitor for a match.

00:05:30.570 --> 00:05:31.750
- At the time. - Yeah?

00:05:31.750 --> 00:05:33.930
The fourth case we've had just like this in a month,

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:36.230
clues are all the same, and they leave no place.

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:37.330
I think we've got a clue.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:38.170
What do you mean?

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.180
Has the fact taken residence in your cranium

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:41.760
that Garfield is the stockbroker,

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.760
and that the other three victims of the recent murder cases

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.060
were also stockbrokers?

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:46.130
Where does that get him?

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:49.000
Why, it gives us a tip on the kind of man to look for.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.610
Instead of wasting your time on thugs,

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.110
you can concentrate on the clients,

00:05:52.110 --> 00:05:54.210
the Garfield, and the other stockbrokers.

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:56.720
Say, if we're going to suspect everybody in this country

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.220
that's sore on stockbrokers,

00:05:58.220 --> 00:06:00.000
we're going to be getting out indictments for...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.990
half the population in the United States.

00:06:02.990 --> 00:06:06.620
Hogan's right, as usual.

00:06:06.620 --> 00:06:09.600
You're theorizing's OK, kid, but I'm with the inspector.

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:12.300
We've got to start with Bucci Galacci or Laci--

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:13.480
oh, Louis.

00:06:13.480 --> 00:06:15.660
He got a swell peek at this guy as he left the building.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:16.900
I'm going to take him down to headquarters

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.560
and let him drag an eye through the rogues' gallery.

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:22.750
Rest the men from the homicide squatter here, sir.

00:06:22.750 --> 00:06:23.540
Send him in.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:26.780
All right, boys.

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:27.870
Hi, gang.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:30.000
Doc, let me have a record of your topsy soon

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.720
you can. Get me a complete photographic record of everything. Footprints, fingerprints, the

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:40.400
whole works. Yes, sir. If we fall down on this case, we're through the bunch of us. And

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.980
as for you, Mugg, when you write this up, use only the barest details. I don't want them

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:47.840
to know anything we found out. Say, Sherlock, I can write all you found out on a postage

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:51.540
stamp. And still have room on it to do a dig cell person. Is that so?

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:59.160
Have you seen him?

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:14.140
The opening of the office is shown by the number of socially and financially prominent people who attended.

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.560
The beautiful Mrs. Leland Gray Switzer lent a charming present,

00:07:17.560 --> 00:07:21.560
who was attired in a severe white evening gown covered by a Nerman wrap.

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:26.990
The lovely Miss Myrtle Hempstead was there also, and she wore a gown--

00:07:26.990 --> 00:07:30.000
Running pants, football fleets, and a spray of rhubarb.

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:32.790
And a good time was had by all,

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:34.790
because our worthless reporter from the Chronicle

00:07:34.790 --> 00:07:36.490
favored the gathering by his absence.

00:07:37.050 --> 00:07:38.150
As to what he was wearing,

00:07:38.620 --> 00:07:40.380
he was just wearing on everyone.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.080
How many times have I asked you not to spoil my record?

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:47.920
About as many times as I've asked you to marry me.

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:50.480
Only up and up, Jerry. How about it?

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:52.170
We get a place and raise some chickens?

00:07:52.350 --> 00:07:52.690
What do you say?

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:55.260
Oh, Jack, I have so much to do. Go away.

00:07:55.360 --> 00:07:57.680
There you are. Marry me and I'll do all your work for it.

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.000
I overheard the city editor saying,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.240
this morning that as far as your work was concerned.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.820
Well, you'd better snap into it.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:04.760
- Oh, yeah?

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:05.760
- For you, Miss Green.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:13.370
- Thanks.

00:08:13.370 --> 00:08:18.790
- Love letter, huh?

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.860
- No, it's from Mother.

00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:22.640
Same old thing.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:25.490
Doctors, bills.

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:27.690
I just can't seem to get anywhere.

00:08:27.690 --> 00:08:28.960
- Carrying too much of a load, kid.

00:08:30.670 --> 00:08:32.650
- Why don't you let me help you?

00:08:32.650 --> 00:08:34.680
- No, Jack, I couldn't do that.

00:08:34.680 --> 00:08:36.340
- But if we got married, I have the right to--

00:08:36.340 --> 00:08:38.720
- The right to let me saddle you with all my burdens.

00:08:38.720 --> 00:08:39.640
Is that it?

00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:43.080
- Say, nothing connected with you could be a burden.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:45.050
You know I'm crazy about you.

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.210
- You've got enough on your shoulders

00:08:46.210 --> 00:08:47.550
looking after yourself, Jack.

00:08:47.550 --> 00:08:48.410
- What's the matter?

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:51.260
Don't you think I'm gonna get anyplace?

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:54.920
- Not unless you settle down to some sort of serious work.

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:57.320
You know, you can write a story with more punch and drama

00:08:57.320 --> 00:09:00.000
than anybody else in the game.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.590
It was a waste of time and sensationalism, murder, kidnapper, frame-up, scandal.

00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:07.770
Well, these guys like Winchell don't do so bad.

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:10.560
You're no Winchell, yes.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:14.460
Say, the only difference between me and Winchell is that he's been around more places than I have.

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:16.640
But I'm catching up on him fast.

00:09:17.530 --> 00:09:18.750
Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:25.550
What do you think of the swell little murder we had last night, huh?

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.000
Now, there you are.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.680
- You would think that a murder was a show or something.

00:09:33.680 --> 00:09:35.020
- Well, this one's gonna be a show.

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:37.540
It'll rock the town before it's through.

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:42.930
Well, well, Philo Vance himself, in person.

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.830
- Good morning, Miss Crane.

00:09:44.830 --> 00:09:45.780
- Good morning, Hogan.

00:09:45.780 --> 00:09:47.530
- Little bit out of my precinct this morning,

00:09:47.530 --> 00:09:50.070
but I feel awful good just having solved

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.610
the Garfield murder.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:52.550
- You solved it?

00:09:52.550 --> 00:09:55.030
Why, you credit-snatching Flatfoot.

00:09:55.030 --> 00:09:56.660
You know it was my idea to have the janitor

00:09:56.660 --> 00:09:58.300
look through the pictures in the morgue room.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:00.000
- Say, if I hadn't agreed to

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:04.740
Kibbutzhi, Batshi, Louie's comin' down to the Chronicle.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.540
What good would your hunch have been?

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:10.490
- You mean they've caught Garfield's murderer?

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:12.620
- Yeah, got him in jail now.

00:10:12.620 --> 00:10:14.740
- Congratulations, Mr. Holgren.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.200
That was pretty quick work, wasn't it?

00:10:16.200 --> 00:10:17.420
- Well, listen to me, that had that guy in the

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.640
"Who's Gow" six hours sooner.

00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:21.790
- Say, what's the matter with you?

00:10:21.790 --> 00:10:23.390
I come down here to tip you off to a scoop

00:10:23.390 --> 00:10:25.390
and you treat me like a relative or something.

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.030
- All right, how much is it gonna cost me?

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:30.000
That is, outside my car, on my next year's salary.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.350
I just wanted to see that you got the name right.

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:35.190
Terrence Aloysius Hogan.

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:36.760
All right, Hogan.

00:10:37.270 --> 00:10:38.110
You're not a bad guy.

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:40.960
I guess you deserve a break.

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:43.080
Yeah, spell that little name right.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:46.040
It's A-L-O-Y-S-I-U-S.

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:46.960
Aloysius.

00:10:47.630 --> 00:10:48.030
Sure.

00:10:48.150 --> 00:10:48.870
How did I miss it?

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:50.520
Aloysius.

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:53.390
On the way down, I picked up a picture.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:55.480
Thought you might need it.

00:10:55.560 --> 00:10:56.680
Oh, yeah, I forgot about that.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:58.950
I could have got one from the morgue room, but probably there's a later picture.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:00.000
Yeah, that's a later one.

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:05.060
Say, what is this?

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:07.570
I thought you were going to give me a picture of the murderer.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:09.940
Why, if we print this,

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.200
they'll prosecute us for libel in the human race.

00:11:16.370 --> 00:11:18.210
Why don't you let Miss Crane run it in the society cover?

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:19.170
He's insane.

00:11:19.570 --> 00:11:20.290
Say, listen.

00:11:21.630 --> 00:11:23.110
When Riley hears about this,

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.730
you won't get within ten miles of the next murder.

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.000
All this guy does is run around solving crime

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.610
and taking the credit away from the police.

00:11:36.610 --> 00:11:38.010
- Poor Hogan.

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:43.570
- He means well, but he just ain't bright.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:47.810
Say, kid, what do you know about this big shot Breen anyway?

00:11:47.810 --> 00:11:48.810
- You mean Jerome Breen?

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:49.770
- Yeah, that's the baby.

00:11:49.770 --> 00:11:52.170
He'd been giving the socialites an awful play lately.

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:54.790
More on your line than mine.

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.970
- I haven't got time to look him up in the files just now.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:00.000
Let me see.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.180
about, oh, about two years ago.

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:04.620
Bought the Tucker estate.

00:12:04.620 --> 00:12:06.290
Everybody likes him.

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:07.890
He's given thousands of dollars to charity

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.410
and all that sort of thing.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:12.680
Boy, with a yarn for the front page, this'll make.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:15.220
What's Freeman doing to get himself from the front page?

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.060
Oh, he's the guy that killed Garfield

00:12:17.060 --> 00:12:19.170
and a few others, probably.

00:12:19.170 --> 00:12:21.020
Jack, that's ridiculous.

00:12:21.020 --> 00:12:22.320
Jerome Breen isn't the sort of man

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:23.540
who would murder anyone.

00:12:23.540 --> 00:12:24.510
No.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:26.510
Well, Louis the janitor possibly identified him

00:12:26.510 --> 00:12:28.470
as the man who was prowling around the building

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:30.000
the night Garfield was strangled.

00:12:30.600 --> 00:12:31.520
Have you seen Mr. Breen?

00:12:31.870 --> 00:12:32.610
What does he say?

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.940
Sure, I was with the squad when they picked him up.

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:36.870
Naturally denies it, they always do.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:41.470
Jack, I think it's terrible to arrest a prominent man like Mr. Breen and have so little evidence.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:42.070
Yeah?

00:12:42.940 --> 00:12:47.210
Well, we're going to have a whole lot more evidence before we get through.

00:12:48.770 --> 00:12:50.350
Now I see it.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:54.210
Just because you and the police department have a lot of unsolved murders on your hands,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.160
you're going to pin it on a man who's done more for this city than all the police and the newspapers put together.

00:12:59.200 --> 00:12:59.670
Say...

00:13:00.730 --> 00:13:02.970
- Why are you so hot for this fellow Breen anyway?

00:13:02.970 --> 00:13:04.630
- Because I know all the good he's done.

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:09.050
His donation to charity, orphanages, hospitals.

00:13:09.050 --> 00:13:11.490
- Yeah, that's a new gag nowadays.

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:16.800
Charity covers a multitude of murders.

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:18.940
- It says here, the man the youngster identified

00:13:18.940 --> 00:13:20.530
spoke to him, right?

00:13:20.530 --> 00:13:22.670
- Right.

00:13:22.670 --> 00:13:23.670
- Well, perhaps you're forgetting the fact

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.370
that Jerome Breen is both deaf and dumb.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:27.010
- No, no, I know all about that.

00:13:27.010 --> 00:13:28.520
I interviewed him once.

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.000
I had a hunch that he could do something with those hands

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:31.970
I'd talk with them.

00:13:31.970 --> 00:13:34.830
- Jack, can't you see how your work is warping your mind?

00:13:34.830 --> 00:13:36.410
Why, all you're becoming is a trafficker

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:38.030
and ruined reputation.

00:13:38.030 --> 00:13:40.810
- Yep, me and Simon agree.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:44.570
You see, it all comes back to what I said in the first place.

00:13:44.570 --> 00:13:45.490
We're always arguing.

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:47.230
We can never agree on anything.

00:13:47.230 --> 00:13:49.710
The only thing for us to do is to get married.

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.490
- With your cruel attitude towards people,

00:13:51.490 --> 00:13:54.050
I wouldn't marry you if you were the last man in the world.

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:56.500
- Baby, if I were the last man in the world,

00:13:56.500 --> 00:13:57.840
I'll say you wouldn't marry me.

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:00.000
You'd be run over in the stampede.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:30.000
Evidently the prosecuting attorney is considering

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.040
worried.

00:14:33.040 --> 00:15:00.000
Mr. Breen says that if

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:08.280
If the prosecuting attorney has any hopes of winning the case, he should be worried.

00:15:32.300 --> 00:15:34.160
That guy can't hear what people are saying to him.

00:15:34.240 --> 00:15:35.060
I'll eat my head.

00:15:35.320 --> 00:15:36.930
Oh, don't be silly, Jack.

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:39.150
Why, all intelligent deaf people are lip readers.

00:15:39.410 --> 00:15:39.770
Oh, yeah?

00:15:41.490 --> 00:15:42.450
What do you think, Chief?

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:43.450
Will they get a conviction?

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:44.910
I don't know, Hogan.

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:45.450
It's got me.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:48.370
All the state has to go on is the testimony of this janitor.

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:55.270
And every woman in town believes he's innocent.

00:15:55.720 --> 00:15:57.540
Well, I was talking to the president who believes his auxiliary.

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:00.000
And even she said that this trial was nothing more than

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.400
- Persecution. - Well, I don't blame them.

00:16:02.400 --> 00:16:06.370
They've been reading those soap articles years about poor Mr. Breen.

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:10.110
What about your stories? Calling them that thinks and everything?

00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:13.750
Well, I...

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:15.400
Hey, hey, hey, hey, hey.

